## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bitwise folding to compute parity without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parity via XOR folding
- Bitwise shifts to mix bits
- No branches or loops in parity path

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t parity32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t p = n;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity32 for 64-bit inputs efficiently?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using a popcount-based method?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying known bit-hacks to multi-lane data without branching, simulating performance-critical kernels.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting across lanes
- 128-bit to four 32-bit lanes mapping
- Branchless arithmetic
- Endianness and lane indexing

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}

uint32_t popcountPerLane128(uint128 x) {
  uint32_t lo0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t lo1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t hi0 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t hi1 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);

  uint32_t p0 = popcount32(lo0);
  uint32_t p1 = popcount32(lo1);
  uint32_t p2 = popcount32(hi0);
  uint32_t p3 = popcount32(hi1);

  return (p3 << 24) | (p2 << 16) | (p1 << 8) | p0;
}
```

